**Solution Explanation**

For a 2 × 2 board we write the four cells in row major order  

```
0 1          a[0] a[1]
2 3   →   a[2] a[3]
```

`a[i]` is a digit `0 … 9`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.   Moves  

*rotate a row* – the two cells of the row are exchanged  

```
row 0 : swap a[0] , a[1]          row 1 : swap a[2] , a[3]
```

*rotate a column* – the two cells of the column are exchanged  

```
col 0 : swap a[0] , a[2]          col 1 : swap a[1] , a[3]
```

Because a row/column contains only two cells “left/right” or “up/down”
produce the same board, therefore there are **exactly four different moves**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.   State representation  

A board is a 4‑digit number  

```
code = a[0]·1000 + a[1]·100 + a[2]·10 + a[3]          (0 … 9999)
```

`code` is used as an index in an array `dist[10000]`.  
`dist[x] = -1`  … not visited yet, otherwise the distance (number of moves)
from the start board.

Conversion functions

```
encode( a[4] ) → int
decode( int code ) → a[4]
```

Both are O(1).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.   Breadth First Search limited to depth 10  

The whole state space contains only `10⁴ = 10000` boards, therefore a normal
BFS is more than fast enough.

```
queue ← start
dist[start] = 0
while queue not empty
        v ← pop
        if v == goal               → answer = dist[v]   (minimal)
        if dist[v] == 10           → continue   // do not expand deeper
        for each of the 4 swaps
                w ← board after the swap
                if dist[w] == -1
                        dist[w] = dist[v] + 1
                        push w
```

If the goal is never taken out of the queue, it cannot be reached in ≤ 10
moves → answer = –1.

The BFS explores at most `4¹⁰ = 1 048 576` nodes, but because the whole graph
has only 10000 vertices the real number of visited vertices is ≤ 10000.
Time   : `O( number of visited states )   ≤ 10000` per test case  
Memory : `dist[10000]` + queue  ≤  a few KB.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.   Input  

Each test case is one line that contains the eight digits of the start board
followed by the eight digits of the goal board (spaces may be present).
All non‑digit characters are ignored.  
If a line yields fewer than eight digits it is skipped.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.   Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm prints the required minimal number of moves,
or –1 when it is impossible within ten moves.

---

##### Lemma 1  
The four defined swaps are exactly the boards reachable by one legal move
(row left/right, column up/down).

**Proof.**  
A row consists of two cells, rotating left moves the left cell to the right
position and the right cell to the left – this is a swap of the two cells.
The same holds for rotating right.  
Analogously a column rotation swaps its two cells.  
No other cells are affected, therefore the four swaps listed above are
precisely the result of one legal move. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
During BFS `dist[x]` equals the length of the shortest sequence of legal moves
that transforms the start board into board `x`.

**Proof.**  
BFS explores states in non‑decreasing order of distance from the start.
When a state `x` is first taken out of the queue, all states with a shorter
distance have already been processed, and `x` is reached from its predecessor
by exactly one additional move.  
Thus the first time we assign a value to `dist[x]` it is the length of a
shortest path from the start to `x`. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
If the goal board is reachable in ≤ 10 moves, the algorithm will output that
minimal number of moves.

**Proof.**  
Assume the goal `g` is reachable in `k ≤ 10` moves.
By Lemma&nbsp;2 the BFS will assign `dist[g] = k` when `g` is first dequeued.
Because the BFS expands all vertices with distance `< k` before any vertex
with distance `k`, `g` will be dequeued exactly after all vertices of distance
`< k` have been processed, i.e. before the algorithm stops expanding at depth
10. The algorithm returns `dist[g] = k`. ∎



##### Lemma 4  
If the algorithm outputs –1, the goal board cannot be reached in ≤ 10 moves.

**Proof.**  
The algorithm stops expanding a vertex whose distance already equals 10.
Consequently every board that can be reached in at most 10 moves from the start
has been visited (otherwise it would be discovered from a vertex of distance
≤ 9).  
If the goal were reachable in ≤ 10 moves it would have been visited and its
distance would be known, contradicting the output –1. ∎



##### Theorem  
For each test case the program prints  

* the minimal number of legal moves that transforms the start board into the
  goal board, if this number is ≤ 10;
* –1 otherwise.

**Proof.**  
If the goal is reachable within ten moves, Lemma&nbsp;3 shows the program
outputs the exact minimal number.  
If the program outputs –1, Lemma&nbsp;4 guarantees that no sequence of ≤ 10
legal moves exists.  
Both cases together give the statement of the theorem. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 6.   Complexity Analysis  

*State space* : 10 000 boards  

*BFS*  

```
time   : O( visited states ) ≤ 10 000          (worst case)
memory : dist[10 000] + queue ≤ 10 000 integers ≈ 40 KB
```

Both limits are negligible for the given problem.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 7.   Reference Implementation  (GNU‑C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/* ---------- encoding / decoding ---------- */
inline int encode(const array<int,4>& a) {
    return a[0]*1000 + a[1]*100 + a[2]*10 + a[3];
}
inline array<int,4> decode(int code) {
    return { code/1000,
             (code/100)%10,
             (code/10)%10,
             code%10 };
}

/* ---------- generate neighbours (the four swaps) ---------- */
static const int swapPairs[4][2] = { {0,1}, {2,3}, {0,2}, {1,3} };

vector<int> neighbours(int code) {
    array<int,4> a = decode(code);
    vector<int> nb;
    nb.reserve(4);
    for (auto &p : swapPairs) {
        array<int,4> b = a;
        swap(b[p[0]], b[p[1]]);
        nb.push_back(encode(b));
    }
    return nb;
}

/* ---------- BFS limited to depth 10 ---------- */
int bfs(int start, int goal) {
    if (start == goal) return 0;
    const int MAXS = 10000;
    static int dist[MAXS];
    fill(begin(dist), end(dist), -1);
    queue<int> q;
    dist[start] = 0;
    q.push(start);

    while (!q.empty()) {
        int v = q.front(); q.pop();
        int d = dist[v];
        if (v == goal) return d;               // minimal distance found
        if (d == 10) continue;                 // do not go deeper
        for (int w : neighbours(v)) {
            if (dist[w] == -1) {
                dist[w] = d + 1;
                q.push(w);
            }
        }
    }
    return -1;                                 // not reachable within 10 moves
}

/* ---------- main ---------- */
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    string line;
    while (getline(cin, line)) {
        vector<int> digits;
        for (char ch : line)
            if (isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(ch)))
                digits.push_back(ch - '0');

        if (digits.size() < 8) continue;          // ignore empty / malformed lines
        // first 4 digits = start, next 4 = goal
        array<int,4> s, g;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) s[i] = digits[i];
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) g[i] = digits[4 + i];

        int start = encode(s);
        int goal  = encode(g);
        int ans = bfs(start, goal);
        cout << ans << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms
to the GNU++17 compiler.

