Below is a **complete, production‑ready “Create User” flow** that ties together:

1. **DTO / Input validation** – guarantees we only get the data we expect.  
2. **Domain model** – the `User` entity that knows how to hash its own password.  
3. **Repository contract** – an abstraction that lets you swap the persistence layer (SQL, NoSQL, in‑memory, etc.) without touching the use‑case.  
4. **Use‑case (application service)** – orchestrates everything: validation → duplicate‑check → password hashing → persistence → output mapping.  
5. **Error handling** – typed errors that surface the right HTTP status codes (or any other transport‑layer mapping).  
6. **Unit‑test skeleton** – shows how to test the use‑case in isolation.

> **Why this structure?**  
> *Clean Architecture* (or *Hexagonal*) keeps **business rules** (the `User` entity and the `CreateUserUseCase`) independent from **frameworks** (Express, Nest, etc.) and **infrastructure** (database, hashing library). That makes the code **testable**, **maintainable**, and **easy to evolve**.

---

## 1️⃣ Domain – `User` Entity

```ts
// src/domain/entities/User.ts
import { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';
import bcrypt from 'bcrypt';

export type UserRole = 'admin' | 'member' | 'guest';

export interface UserProps {
  id?: string;          // generated automatically if omitted
  name: string;
  email: string;
  password: string;     // **plain** password when constructing, will be hashed
  role: UserRole;
  createdAt?: Date;
  updatedAt?: Date;
}

/**
 * The User entity encapsulates all invariants that belong to a user.
 * It knows how to hash its own password and can expose a safe DTO
 * (without the password hash) for the outer world.
 */
export class User {
  public readonly id: string;
  public name: string;
  public email: string;
  private _passwordHash: string;
  public role: UserRole;
  public readonly createdAt: Date;
  public updatedAt: Date;

  private constructor(props: UserProps, passwordHash: string) {
    this.id = props.id ?? uuidv4();
    this.name = props.name;
    this.email = props.email.toLowerCase(); // enforce canonical email
    this._passwordHash = passwordHash;
    this.role = props.role;
    this.createdAt = props.createdAt ?? new Date();
    this.updatedAt = props.updatedAt ?? new Date();
  }

  /** Factory – validates & hashes the password */
  public static async create(props: UserProps): Promise<User> {
    // ---- 1️⃣ Basic validation (you can replace with class‑validator, zod, etc.) ----
    if (!props.name?.trim()) throw new InvalidUserDataError('Name is required');
    if (!props.email?.trim()) throw new InvalidUserDataError('Email is required');
    if (!props.password?.trim()) throw new InvalidUserDataError('Password is required');
    if (!['admin', 'member', 'guest'].includes(props.role))
      throw new InvalidUserDataError('Invalid role');

    // ---- 2️⃣ Hash password (bcrypt is a safe default) ----
    const saltRounds = 12; // adjust based on your performance/security trade‑off
    const hash = await bcrypt.hash(props.password, saltRounds);

    // ---- 3️⃣ Build the entity (password hash is stored, plain password never lives on the instance) ----
    return new User({ ...props, password: undefined }, hash);
  }

  /** Verify a plain password against the stored hash */
  public async verifyPassword(plain: string): Promise<boolean> {
    return bcrypt.compare(plain, this._passwordHash);
  }

  /** Expose a safe DTO (no password hash) */
  public toDTO(): Omit<UserProps, 'password'> {
    return {
      id: this.id,
      name: this.name,
      email: this.email,
      role: this.role,
      createdAt: this.createdAt,
      updatedAt: this.updatedAt,
    };
  }

  /** For persistence – we need the hash, not the plain password */
  public get passwordHash(): string {
    return this._passwordHash;
  }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Custom domain errors – keep them in the same file or a dedicated folder   */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
export class InvalidUserDataError extends Error {
  public readonly name = 'InvalidUserDataError';
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
  }
}
```

### What the entity does

| Concern | Implementation |
|---------|----------------|
| **Identity** | Generates a UUID if the caller didn’t provide one. |
| **Invariant enforcement** | Throws `InvalidUserDataError` for missing/invalid fields. |
| **Password hashing** | Done **once** in the static `create` factory – guarantees the hash is always present. |
| **Encapsulation** | The raw hash is private; only the entity can verify a password. |
| **DTO conversion** | `toDTO()` strips the hash, perfect for returning from an API. |

---

## 2️⃣ Repository Contract (Port)

```ts
// src/domain/repositories/IUserRepository.ts
import { User } from '../entities/User';

export interface IUserRepository {
  /** Persist a new user – returns the persisted entity (or its ID). */
  save(user: User): Promise<User>;

  /** Find a user by e‑mail – used to enforce uniqueness. */
  findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null>;

  /** (Optional) Find by ID – useful for other use‑cases. */
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
}
```

> **Why an interface?**  
> It decouples the use‑case from the concrete DB (e.g., Prisma, TypeORM, MongoDB, in‑memory). You can swap implementations without touching business logic.

---

## 3️⃣ Application Service – `CreateUserUseCase`

```ts
// src/application/usecases/CreateUserUseCase.ts
import { IUserRepository } from '../../domain/repositories/IUserRepository';
import { User, InvalidUserDataError } from '../../domain/entities/User';

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Public input DTO – what the outer world (controller, GraphQL resolver…)   */
/* sends to the use‑case.                                                     */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
export interface CreateUserInput {
  name: string;
  email: string;
  password: string;
  role: 'admin' | 'member' | 'guest';
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Output DTO – what the use‑case returns (never the password hash).          */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
export type CreateUserOutput = ReturnType<User['toDTO']>;

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Domain‑specific error that the controller can translate to HTTP 409.       */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
export class EmailAlreadyTakenError extends Error {
  public readonly name = 'EmailAlreadyTakenError';
  constructor(email: string) {
    super(`The e‑mail "${email}" is already registered`);
  }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* The use‑case itself – pure business logic, no framework code.              */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
export class CreateUserUseCase {
  constructor(private readonly userRepo: IUserRepository) {}

  /** Executes the flow: validation → duplicate check → hashing → persistence */
  public async execute(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<CreateUserOutput> {
    // 1️⃣ Normalise e‑mail early (lower‑case, trim) – prevents case‑sensitive dupes
    const email = input.email.trim().toLowerCase();

    // 2️⃣ Guard against duplicate e‑mail
    const existing = await this.userRepo.findByEmail(email);
    if (existing) throw new EmailAlreadyTakenError(email);

    // 3️⃣ Build the domain entity (hashing happens inside the factory)
    const user = await User.create({
      name: input.name.trim(),
      email,
      password: input.password,
      role: input.role,
    });

    // 4️⃣ Persist – the repository decides what to store (hash, timestamps, etc.)
    const persisted = await this.userRepo.save(user);

    // 5️⃣ Return a safe DTO (no password hash)
    return persisted.toDTO();
  }
}
```

### How the use‑case stays **framework‑agnostic**

| Step | What it does | No framework dependency |
|------|--------------|------------------------|
| Normalise e‑mail | `trim().toLowerCase()` | Pure JS |
| Duplicate check | Calls `userRepo.findByEmail` | Repository is an interface |
| Entity creation | `User.create` (hashes password) | Uses only `bcrypt` (a pure lib) |
| Persistence | `userRepo.save` | Interface again |
| Output mapping | `toDTO()` | Pure method on the entity |

---

## 4️⃣ Infrastructure – Example Repository (Prisma)

> *You can replace this with any ORM/ODM. The only contract you must respect is `IUserRepository`.*

```ts
// src/infrastructure/prisma/PrismaUserRepository.ts
import { PrismaClient, User as PrismaUser } from '@prisma/client';
import { IUserRepository } from '../../domain/repositories/IUserRepository';
import { User } from '../../domain/entities/User';

export class PrismaUserRepository implements IUserRepository {
  constructor(private readonly prisma: PrismaClient) {}

  async save(user: User): Promise<User> {
    const data = {
      id: user.id,
      name: user.name,
      email: user.email,
      passwordHash: user.passwordHash,
      role: user.role,
      createdAt: user.createdAt,
      updatedAt: new Date(),
    };

    // Upsert works for both create & update (here we only create)
    await this.prisma.user.upsert({
      where: { id: user.id },
      create: data,
      update: data,
    });

    // Return the same entity (or you could re‑fetch to get DB defaults)
    return user;
  }

  async findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null> {
    const prismaUser = await this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
    return prismaUser ? this.fromPrisma(prismaUser) : null;
  }

  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
    const prismaUser = await this.prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
    return prismaUser ? this.fromPrisma(prismaUser) : null;
  }

  /** Helper – map Prisma row → domain entity (no hashing needed) */
  private fromPrisma(prismaUser: PrismaUser): User {
    // NOTE: we bypass the factory because the password is already hashed.
    const user = new User(
      {
        id: prismaUser.id,
        name: prismaUser.name,
        email: prismaUser.email,
        password: undefined as any, // placeholder – not used
        role: prismaUser.role as any,
        createdAt: prismaUser.createdAt,
        updatedAt: prismaUser.updatedAt,
      },
      prismaUser.passwordHash,
    );
    return user;
  }
}
```

> **Tip:** If you use **NestJS**, you can inject `PrismaUserRepository` as a provider that implements `IUserRepository`. The same goes for Express, Fastify, etc.

---

## 5️⃣ Presentation Layer – Minimal Express Example

```ts
// src/interfaces/http/express.ts
import express, { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
import { PrismaUserRepository } from '../infrastructure/prisma/PrismaUserRepository';
import { CreateUserUseCase, EmailAlreadyTakenError, InvalidUserDataError } from '../application/usecases/CreateUserUseCase';

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

/* Dependency wiring – in a real app you’d use a DI container */
const prisma = new PrismaClient();
const userRepo = new PrismaUserRepository(prisma);
const createUser = new CreateUserUseCase(userRepo);

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Route handler – thin, only translates HTTP ↔️ use‑case                     */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
app.post('/users', async (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
  try {
    const output = await createUser.execute(req.body);
    res.status(201).json(output);
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof EmailAlreadyTakenError) {
      return res.status(409).json({ error: err.message });
    }
    if (err instanceof InvalidUserDataError) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: err.message });
    }
    // Unexpected – let the generic error‑handler deal with it
    next(err);
  }
});

/* Generic error handler (optional) */
app.use((err: Error, _req: Request, res: Response, _next: NextFunction) => {
  console.error(err);
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal Server Error' });
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('🚀 API listening on http://localhost:3000'));
```

**Result:**  
`POST /users` with a JSON body:

```json
{
  "name": "Ada Lovelace",
  "email": "Ada@example.com",
  "password": "SuperSecret123!",
  "role": "admin"
}
```

returns (on success, HTTP 201):

```json
{
  "id": "c2a1b5e2-7c3d-4f9a-9c2b-5e6d7f8a9b0c",
  "name": "Ada Lovelace",
  "email": "ada@example.com",
  "role": "admin",
  "createdAt": "2024-09-23T12:34:56.789Z",
  "updatedAt": "2024-09-23T12:34:56.789Z"
}
```

No password hash ever leaves the server.

