**Solution Explanation**

For a string `S`

* a **substring** is a contiguous block `S[l … r]` (`0 ≤ l ≤ r < N`);
* a **subsequence** is any sequence that can be obtained from `S` by deleting
  characters, i.e. the order of the kept characters must be the same as in `S`.

Every substring is trivially a subsequence (just keep the characters of the
substring and delete everything else).  
Therefore a substring is a subsequence **iff** the substring itself occurs in `S`,
which is always true.

Consequently the problem asks for the number of **different** substrings of `S`
(the word *different* is implicit – otherwise the answer would be the trivial
`N·(N+1)/2`).  
So we have to count how many distinct strings appear as a contiguous block in
`S`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.  Counting distinct substrings with a Suffix Automaton  

A *suffix automaton* (SAM) of a string `S` is a deterministic finite automaton
that recognises **all** substrings of `S`.  
Each state `v` of the SAM represents a set of substrings whose lengths form a
continuous interval

```
len[link[v]] + 1 … len[v]          (len[link[root]] = 0)
```

where  

* `len[v]` – the length of the longest substring represented by `v`;
* `link[v]` – the suffix‑link (the state that represents the longest proper
  suffix of the substrings of `v`).

All substrings represented by `v` are different from the substrings represented
by any other state, and the intervals of different states are disjoint.
Hence the number of **distinct** substrings equals the sum, over all states,
of the size of their interval:

```
answer = Σ ( len[v] – len[link[v]] )      over all states v ≠ root
```

The SAM can be built online in `O(N)` time by processing the characters of `S`
one by one.  
The number of states is at most `2·N`, therefore the whole algorithm works in
linear time and memory.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.  Algorithm
```
build SAM of S
ans = 0
for every state v (except the root)
        ans += len[v] - len[link[v]]
output ans
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.  Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm returns exactly the number of distinct substrings
of `S`.

---

##### Lemma 1  
For a state `v` of the suffix automaton, the set of substrings represented by
`v` consists of **all** substrings of `S` whose lengths belong to the interval
`( len[link[v]] , len[v] ]` and whose last character ends at the same position
in `S` as the longest one (`len[v]`).

**Proof.**  
Construction of the SAM guarantees that every path from the root spells a
substring of `S`.  
When a new character is added, the algorithm creates (or re‑uses) a state whose
`len` equals the length of the longest substring ending at the current position.
The suffix‑link of that state points to the state that corresponds to the longest
proper suffix of those substrings, i.e. to the state with maximal length
`len[link[v]]`.  
All intermediate lengths are also present because the automaton contains a
transition for each character of the longest substring, thus every length
between `len[link[v]]+1` and `len[v]` is reachable and corresponds to a distinct
substring. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
The intervals `( len[link[v]] , len[v] ]` of different states `v` are pairwise
disjoint.

**Proof.**  
Assume two different states `v₁` and `v₂` share a length `L`.  
Then there would be two different ways to reach a substring of length `L`
ending at the same position, which contradicts the definition of the SAM:
for each length and each end position there is exactly one state that
represents the set of substrings with that maximal length. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
The number of distinct substrings of `S` equals  

```
Σ ( len[v] – len[link[v]] )   over all states v ≠ root .
```

**Proof.**  
By Lemma&nbsp;1, state `v` contributes exactly the `len[v] – len[link[v]]`
different lengths of substrings that belong to it.
By Lemma&nbsp;2 these contributions are disjoint, i.e. no substring is counted
twice.  
All substrings appear in some state (the state that corresponds to the longest
occurrence), therefore the sum counts **every** distinct substring exactly once. ∎



##### Lemma 4  
The algorithm computes the sum described in Lemma&nbsp;3.

**Proof.**  
After the SAM is built, the algorithm iterates over all states except the root
and adds `len[v] – len[link[v]]` to the answer. This is precisely the sum of
Lemma&nbsp;3. ∎



##### Theorem  
The algorithm outputs the number of distinct substrings of the given string
`S`.

**Proof.**  
By Lemma&nbsp;4 the algorithm outputs the sum of Lemma&nbsp;3.
By Lemma&nbsp;3 this sum equals the number of distinct substrings.
Therefore the algorithm is correct. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.  Complexity Analysis  

*Building the SAM* – `O(N)` time, at most `2·N` states, each with a constant
number of fields → `O(N)` memory.  

*Summation* – one linear pass over the states → `O(N)` time.  

Overall  

```
Time   : O(N)
Memory : O(N)
```

Both bounds satisfy the usual limits (`N` up to 10⁵ … 10⁶).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.  Reference Implementation  (C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct SAM {
    struct State {
        int next[26];
        int link;          // suffix link
        int len;           // longest length
        State() {
            fill(begin(next), end(next), -1);
            link = -1;
            len = 0;
        }
    };
    vector<State> st;
    int last;              // state of the whole string processed so far

    SAM(int maxLen = 0) {          // reserve enough space
        st.reserve(2 * maxLen);
        st.push_back(State());     // root = 0
        st[0].len = 0;
        st[0].link = -1;
        last = 0;
    }

    void extend(char ch) {
        int c = ch - 'a';
        int cur = (int)st.size();
        st.push_back(State());
        st[cur].len = st[last].len + 1;

        int p = last;
        while (p != -1 && st[p].next[c] == -1) {
            st[p].next[c] = cur;
            p = st[p].link;
        }

        if (p == -1) {
            st[cur].link = 0;               // link to root
        } else {
            int q = st[p].next[c];
            if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                st[cur].link = q;
            } else {
                // clone state q
                int clone = (int)st.size();
                st.push_back(st[q]);        // copy all fields
                st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                // fix links
                while (p != -1 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                    st[p].next[c] = clone;
                    p = st[p].link;
                }
                st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
            }
        }
        last = cur;
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    string S;
    if (!(cin >> S)) return 0;
    int N = (int)S.size();

    SAM sam(N);
    for (char c : S) sam.extend(c);

    long long ans = 0;
    for (size_t v = 1; v < sam.st.size(); ++v) {   // skip root (v = 0)
        int link = sam.st[v].link;
        int linkLen = (link == -1 ? 0 : sam.st[link].len);
        ans += (long long)(sam.st[v].len - linkLen);
    }

    cout << ans << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms
to the GNU++17 compiler.

