You can let the **System.IO** APIs do the heavy lifting for you.  
Both `Directory.GetFiles` (returns an array) and `Directory.EnumerateFiles` (returns an `IEnumerable<string>`) accept a **search‑option** that tells the runtime to walk the whole directory tree.

---

## 1. One‑liner with `Directory.GetFiles`

```csharp
using System.IO;

// root folder you want to scan
string root = @"C:\MyFolder";

// all files, any extension, in the folder *and* every sub‑folder
string[] allFiles = Directory.GetFiles(root, "*.*", SearchOption.AllDirectories);
```

* `*.*` – pattern; change it to `"*.txt"` or `"*"` if you want everything.  
* `SearchOption.AllDirectories` – tells the method to recurse into sub‑folders.

> **Note** – `GetFiles` builds the entire array in memory before returning it.  
> For a very large tree this can be expensive.

---

## 2. Streaming version with `Directory.EnumerateFiles`

If you prefer a lazy, memory‑friendly approach (e.g. you’ll process the files one‑by‑one), use `EnumerateFiles`:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Collections.Generic;

IEnumerable<string> allFiles = Directory.EnumerateFiles(
    root,
    "*.*",
    SearchOption.AllDirectories);

foreach (string filePath in allFiles)
{
    // Do something with each file
    Console.WriteLine(filePath);
}
```

`EnumerateFiles` yields each file as soon as it’s discovered, so you never have to hold the whole list in RAM.

---

## 3. Handling access‑rights problems

When walking a directory tree you can hit folders you don’t have permission to read.  
If you want the operation to continue rather than throw, wrap the enumeration in a try/catch (or write a small helper that swallows the exception for the offending folder).

```csharp
IEnumerable<string> GetAllFiles(string path)
{
    var files = new List<string>();

    try
    {
        files.AddRange(Directory.GetFiles(path));

        foreach (string subDir in Directory.GetDirectories(path))
        {
            files.AddRange(GetAllFiles(subDir));   // recursive call
        }
    }
    catch (UnauthorizedAccessException)
    {
        // skip folders we cannot access
    }

    return files;
}
```

Or, using the streaming API with a custom iterator:

```csharp
IEnumerable<string> SafeEnumerateFiles(string path)
{
    Stack<string> dirs = new Stack<string>();
    dirs.Push(path);

    while (dirs.Count > 0)
    {
        string current = dirs.Pop();
        string[] subDirs = Array.Empty<string>();
        string[] files   = Array.Empty<string>();

        try { subDirs = Directory.GetDirectories(current); }
        catch (UnauthorizedAccessException) { /* ignore */ }

        try { files = Directory.GetFiles(current); }
        catch (UnauthorizedAccessException) { /* ignore */ }

        foreach (var f in files)   yield return f;
        foreach (var d in subDirs) dirs.Push(d);
    }
}
```

---

## 4. Quick recap of the options

| API                     | Returns                     | When to use                                   |
|-------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `Directory.GetFiles`    | `string[]` (full array)    | Small‑to‑medium trees, you need the whole list at once |
| `Directory.EnumerateFiles` | `IEnumerable<string>` (lazy) | Large trees, streaming processing, lower memory footprint |
| Custom recursive method| `IEnumerable<string>` (you control error handling) | Need fine‑grained control over exceptions or additional filtering |

That’s it – with one line (or a tiny helper) you can retrieve **every file** under a directory, no matter how deep the hierarchy goes. Happy coding!

